# PUTSH — An Open Protocol for Trustworthy Human–AI Collaboration

## Abstract

PUTSH is an open protocol for trustworthy collaboration between humans
and artificial intelligence.

PUTSH defines a common framework for:

- representing knowledge;
- identifying actors;
- assigning work;
- coordinating agents;
- validating results;
- preserving provenance;
- enforcing governance;
- separating experimentation from production.

PUTSH is independent of any particular artificial intelligence model,
provider, programming language, operating system, or cloud platform.

An implementation MAY run entirely locally.

An implementation MAY use cloud services.

An implementation MAY combine multiple AI models.

The protocol remains the same.

## Design Principle

PUTSH separates specification from implementation.

The PUTSH Specifications define the protocol.

Implementations provide executable software.

No implementation is authoritative over the specification.

## Scope

PUTSH is designed for systems in which humans and artificial agents
collaborate over extended periods of time.

The protocol supports:

- individual users;
- communities;
- research laboratories;
- organizations;
- companies;
- public-interest projects;
- autonomous software agents.

## Non-Goals

PUTSH does not define:

- a foundation AI model;
- a proprietary AI service;
- a mandatory cloud infrastructure;
- a single user interface;
- a single programming language;
- a single economic model.

## Core Properties

A conforming PUTSH system SHOULD provide:

1. Human governance.
2. Explicit agent identity.
3. Verifiable provenance.
4. Structured knowledge.
5. Task traceability.
6. Permission control.
7. Separation of environments.
8. Reversible changes.
9. Auditable history.
10. Provider independence.

## Normative Language

The key words MUST, MUST NOT, REQUIRED, SHALL, SHALL NOT,
SHOULD, SHOULD NOT, RECOMMENDED, MAY, and OPTIONAL are to be
interpreted as normative requirements.

## Versioning

PUTSH specifications use semantic versioning.

Breaking protocol changes require a major version.

Backward-compatible additions require a minor version.

Editorial corrections require a patch version.

## Authority

The canonical version of a PUTSH specification is the version
published by the PUTSH Foundation through its designated
publication mechanism.

## Security Principle

No agent, plugin, model, workflow, or implementation may override
the PUTSH Constitution.

Constitutional authority precedes operational authority.

## Foundation

PUTSH is intended to operate as an open technological foundation
whose purpose is to increase humanity's capacity to understand,
coordinate and solve problems of public interest.

The protocol itself does not determine which problems humanity
should solve.

It provides infrastructure through which humans and agents may
address them responsibly.
